    tourTitle: `The Rostovs' name day in Moscow`,
    hook: `Moscow, August 1805. Countess Rostova receives visitors on her daughters' name day; the count circulates warmly in all directions; the house is enormous, hospitable, and cheerfully disordered.`,
    blurb: `Moscow: Countess Rostova's name day reception. Count Rostov invites everyone to dinner with the same phrase. Anna Mikhaylovna arrives with her son Boris and an agenda.`,
    tour: `The novel shifts to Moscow and to the Rostov household — and the tonal shift is immediate and total. The Bezukhov/Bolkonsky world is aristocratic, formal, French. The Rostov world is warm, Russian, chaotic, hospitable to excess. Count Rostov invites every single visitor to dinner with the identical phrase and the same hearty handshake, regardless of their rank. The countess receives in the drawing room, worn out from twelve children. Anna Mikhaylovna Drubetskaya — a former aristocrat now poor and anxious — has brought her son Boris and is maneuvering to get him into the Semeonov Guards through the Rostovs' connections. The household is defined by feeling rather than calculation: the count loves people, the countess loves her children, the house overflows with guests and noise. It is also in debt, though Tolstoy doesn't press that point here. The Rostov world is the emotional counterweight to the St. Petersburg of Anna Pavlovna's salon.`,
    summary: [
      `The scene moves to Moscow on the Rostovs' name day — a double celebration, for both the Countess Natalya and her youngest daughter Natasha. Carriages have been arriving since morning at the Rostovs' large house on the Povarskaya. The countess is a woman in her mid-forties, visibly worn from twelve children but dignified; her eldest daughter sits beside her receiving visitors. Princess Anna Mikhaylovna Drubetskaya has come from Petersburg with her son Boris — she has secured his transfer from a line regiment to the Semenov Guards through Prince Vasili's intervention, and she is now working to get Boris appointed to Kutuzov's staff.`,
      `Count Rostov moves through the reception rooms in a state of continuous warm hospitality: he invites every single guest to dinner using the same words ('I'll be offended if you don't, my dear!') and the same firm handshake, with genuine and indiscriminate pleasure. He is the kind of man who loves people without discriminating among them, who finds the presence of a crowded room simply pleasurable. Tolstoy describes him without irony — this is not the performance of hospitality but its reality.`,
      `The house is introduced as a world: the marble dining room being set for eighty, the conservatory, the study where the men are already gathering with pipes and political talk. Anna Mikhaylovna is poorer than the Rostovs and dependent on their goodwill; she navigates this with the practiced dignity of a woman who has learned to manage her position without letting anyone see the effort. Her campaign to place Boris will wind through several chapters. The Rostov household establishes the novel's second great social world alongside Petersburg — warmer, more Russian, more morally straightforward, and financially much more fragile.`,
    ],
    appears: [{ id: 'countess-rostova', name: "Countess Rostova" }, { id: 'count-rostov', name: "Count Ilya Rostov" }, { id: 'anna-mikhaylovna', name: "Princess Anna Mikhaylovna Drubetskaya" }, { id: 'boris', name: "Boris Drubetskoy" }, { id: 'vera', name: "Vera Rostova" }],
