    n: 195,
    title: `Book Ten (1812) — Chapter V`,
    summary: [
      `Prince Andrei's regiment is marching along the highroad past Bald Hills, his family estate, which he can see from the road but does not visit. The old avenue, the house, the world of his childhood pass at a distance while he rides by in command of his men. Smoke rises from the direction of the village. He does not stop. He gives no order to stop.`,
      `Since Smolensk's fall Andrei has been changed. A new feeling — anger against the enemy — has replaced the older philosophical detachment he cultivated after Austerlitz. The management of his regiment absorbs him in a way that his private speculations never did. He cares about the welfare of his men. He is good at command, and knowing this gives him something to stand on.`,
      `Everything still appears to him in a dark and gloomy light. He believes Smolensk could and should have been defended. He is angry at the retreat, at the incompetence of the high command, at the abandonment of his father's estate. His father has had to flee to Moscow. The estate will be pillaged. The familiar avenue disappears behind him as the regiment moves on.`,
    ],
