    n: 199,
    title: `Book Ten (1812) — Chapter IX`,
    summary: [
      `Tolstoy describes the Bogucharovo peasants as distinct in character from the Bald Hills serfs. They are steppe peasants — boorish, autonomous, accustomed to absentee landlords and to doing more or less as they pleased. Andrei's reforms, when he owned Bogucharovo, had reduced their quitrent and introduced schools and hospitals, but this had not softened them; if anything it had strengthened their independence and confirmed their sense that they could negotiate from a position.`,
      `Various rumors circulate among these peasants about the French and about what the French will offer. The idea has taken hold in some villages that the French will free the serfs. Whether this is true matters less than that it is believed. The Bogucharovo peasants are watching events closely and drawing conclusions about where their interests lie. The arrival of the French is not obviously the worst outcome they can imagine.`,
      `Alpatyich, sent to the town for provisions, cannot get what he needs. The town itself is half evacuated. He finds roads clogged with retreating troops and carts. He brings back whatever he can. The situation at Bald Hills and Bogucharovo is deteriorating. The estates lie directly in the path of the French advance. Marya, alone after her father's death, is the sole authority over a household that may or may not follow her orders.`,
    ],
