    n: 200,
    title: `Book Ten (1812) — Chapter X`,
    summary: [
      `After the old prince's funeral Marya shuts herself in her room. She cannot think coherently about practical matters. She lies facing the wall, fingering the leather cushion, absorbed in thoughts about death's irrevocability and her own spiritual condition. The sun shifts across the room. Outside, carriages and carts are being loaded for departure. She does not give orders.`,
      `Dron, the estate manager, comes to her and tells her — gently, reluctantly — that the Bogucharovo peasants will not provide horses. They say they need the horses themselves. This means the household cannot leave. Marya does not fully understand the implications. She asks Alpatyich to arrange it. Dron says Alpatyich cannot arrange it. She asks Dron to arrange it himself. He asks to be discharged — after twenty-three years of service, he asks simply to be let go.`,
      `Marya does not understand why the man who has managed the estate for twenty-three years wants to be discharged at the worst possible moment. She tells him she has never doubted his loyalty and will do anything for him and for the peasants. But what she needs — horses, departure — she cannot seem to obtain. The machinery of the estate, which has run smoothly all her life, has quietly stopped. She cannot see why.`,
    ],
