    n: 201,
    title: `Book Ten (1812) — Chapter XI`,
    summary: [
      `The peasants have assembled at the barn. Dunyasha, the maid, tells Marya that they want to speak with her and warns her not to go out — it's a trick, she says. Marya goes out. She faces a crowd of several hundred serfs. All faces wear the same expression: a complex mixture of curiosity, distrust, gratitude, and something that might be hostility. She cannot read it.`,
      `Marya addresses them. She tells them she has ordered grain to be given out. She tells them the French are coming and that she wants them to help the household leave. She speaks in the gentle, formal manner she has been taught. The crowd listens and then the voices start up from the back — voices saying she means to lead them into slavery, that it's all a trick, that they won't go. Marya repeats her offer. The crowd does not move.`,
      `She returns to the house. She has failed utterly and does not entirely understand how. The peasants have effectively imprisoned the household on the estate by refusing horses. Princess Marya, who has spent her life in submission to her father and to God, finds herself alone and powerless in the face of a crowd of people she was taught to think of as hers. She goes to her room and stays alone with her thoughts.`,
    ],
