    n: 207,
    title: `Book Ten (1812) — Chapter XVII`,
    summary: [
      `Moscow society continues in its usual course. The Emperor has left for the front, there has been a period of patriotic enthusiasm with subscriptions raised and speeches given, but that has subsided and the city has returned to visits, dinner parties, and French-language games at the English Club. It is difficult, Tolstoy notes, to believe that Russia is in danger when the city looks exactly as it has always looked.`,
      `At the approach of any great danger, he observes, two voices always speak: one says think of the danger, act, sacrifice; the other says that the danger is exaggerated and that the sensible man stays calm and looks after himself. In Moscow, by August, the second voice has mostly won. Governor Rostopchins broadsheets — woodcuts of a Moscow burgher named Karpushka Chigirin delivering patriotic speeches in comic dialect — are circulated and read aloud and laughed at, performing patriotism as entertainment.`,
      `Pierre is at a society gathering where the guests play a parlor game requiring them to supply Russian equivalents for French phrases. The game is slightly absurd given that the same people have been speaking French their whole lives and can barely do it. Julie Drubetskaya, who has been corresponding with Marya in a newly patriotic Russian, asks Pierre with a knowing smile about Natasha. Pierre is evidently in an unsettled state. He neither laughs as much as the others nor looks as comfortable.`,
    ],
