    n: 214,
    title: `Book Ten (1812) — Chapter XXIV`,
    summary: [
      `That same evening of August 25, Prince Andrei lies in a broken-down shed at the far end of his regiment's encampment. Through a gap in the wall he can see birch trees, a fence, and the evening sky. He has given his orders for the battle. He has nothing more to do. The thoughts that come are the simplest and therefore the most terrible — about death, about what tomorrow means, about whether what he is about to experience will be worse than Austerlitz.`,
      `He knows tomorrow's battle will be the worst he has taken part in. He knows that for the first time he genuinely fears death. Not as an abstraction or a philosophical proposition — he has worked through those — but as an actual physical ending of the specific existence he inhabits: the existence that remembers Natasha's face, that loves his son, that has its particular habits and memories and irritations. He does not want to stop existing. The knowledge arrives at him without consolation.`,
      `Pierre appears at the shed. The two men talk — awkwardly at first, with staff officers present, then as the others leave, more directly. Andrei asks about Moscow, about his people, whether they have left. He asks about the Masonic brothers and what they say about war, with a touch of the old ironic contempt. Pierre tells him what he has seen that day on the battlefield — the soldiers, the militia, the certainty he has felt that they will win. Andrei listens without sharing Pierre's certainty. He is not, at this moment, thinking about winning.`,
    ],
