    n: 216,
    title: `Book Ten (1812) — Chapter XXVI`,
    summary: [
      `On the eve of Borodino the French imperial machinery is also in motion. M. de Beausset, prefect of Napoleon's palace, arrives from Paris with a box. The box contains a surprise: a portrait of Napoleon's infant son, the King of Rome, painted by Gerard, holding a toy stick with a terrestrial globe on top of it — the child piercing the earth with his scepter. Napoleon is delighted. He arranges for the portrait to be brought out and displayed so his staff can admire it.`,
      `Napoleon poses beside the portrait. He makes remarks about it. His aides respond with appropriate enthusiasm. He says — with the gravity of a man making a pronouncement — that the portrait must not be shown to the troops: 'It is too soon for him to see a field of battle.' De Beausset closes his eyes and bows his head deeply, indicating how profoundly he values this imperial wisdom. The performance is complete on both sides.`,
      `Tolstoy's portrait of Napoleon at Borodino's eve is deliberate reduction. The great man parades his sentimentality about his son, his vanity about his son's symbolism, his management of his own image through every small gesture. None of this activity is connected to the battle about to happen. A hundred thousand men will fight tomorrow. The Emperor poses beside the portrait of his infant boy on a globe and his courtiers express admiration. Tolstoy notes both facts with equal attention and lets the reader do the arithmetic.`,
    ],
