    n: 223,
    title: `Book Ten (1812) — Chapter XXXIII`,
    summary: [
      `A chapter of military analysis and philosophical commentary, stepping back from the scene of Pierre's experience to account for the battle of Borodino as a whole. Tolstoy describes the geography of the engagement: the seven thousand feet between Borodino and the Bagration fleches was the decisive ground; the flanking operations on both sides were demonstrations rather than serious attacks; the main collision happened in the center, at the redoubt, and it was resolved not by maneuver but by attrition.`,
      `The analysis is Tolstoy's own and runs against the grain of every professional military history of the battle. He argues that neither side achieved its tactical objectives and that the battle was decided not by the superior genius of either commander but by the aggregate behavior of hundreds of thousands of individual men, each acting from fear, exhaustion, habit, and the crowd dynamics of the men around him. Campan's and Dessaix's divisions, Murat's cavalry, the attacks on Borodino itself — all are described in terms of what actually happened to them rather than what was ordered to happen.`,
      `The chapter ends with the armies having fought themselves to a standstill: the Russians still in possession of the road to Moscow, the French having advanced but not broken through, both sides unwilling to continue. Tolstoy makes no claim about who won. He argues that the very framing of the question — who won at Borodino — is a retrospective imposition of narrative on an event that had no narrative shape while it was happening. The men who fought it did not know what they were doing; they only knew what was in front of them.`,
    ],
